  "Would fail if two goroutines both succeeded" is demonstrated, not inferred. Third
  scratch copy <scratchpad>/b2mut8c with two edits: the step-3 fast-path Stat block deleted,
  and write bound to an always-succeeding atomic overwrite (CreateTemp + Rename, so there is
  no tearing and no EEXIST). Every goroutine then legitimately imports:
      --- FAIL: TestImportOneFile_ProductionWriteBindingIsExclusive (0.00s)
          import_binding_race_test.go:87: round 0: 16 of 16 goroutines reported imported
          (want exactly 1) — defaultImportDeps().write is no longer exclusive
  The imported!=1 branch fires on over-success, not only on under-success. The Reason-string
  check at :82 is likewise load-bearing: the "skipped" reason is compared for equality, so a
  skip produced by the wrong branch with a different reason would be reported.
  The destination-content assertion is meaningful because all 16 goroutines write identical
  bytes only under the exclusive binding (one write, no partial states); mutant A produced
  "saved file failed verification" precisely because that invariant broke.

CRITERION 6 — deleteSource=false: justified, and the deletion half is covered elsewhere: MET,
  with one narrow residual noted.
  (a) The worker's stated reason (import_binding_race_test.go:54-58) is empirically correct,
      not a convenience. Fourth scratch copy <scratchpad>/b2del: the same test with
      importOneFile("race.csv", true, deps) and the mustExist assertion dropped, production
      code otherwise untouched:
          DELETESOURCE_TRUE_VARIANT: PASS=0 FAIL=10   (10/10 runs fail)
      with outcomes such as
          round 0: unexpected outcome {Name:race.csv Status:rejected Reason:not found in the import folder}  (131 occurrences)
          round 0: 0 goroutines reported skipped, want 15
      The cause is real and unavoidable: the winner's step-6 os.Remove (handlers.go:725)
      races the losers' step-2 os.Lstat (:659) and step-4 deps.readSource (:694), so losers
      are rejected as "not found in the import folder" instead of skipped. With
      deleteSource=true the outcome partition is genuinely nondeterministic. Choosing false
      was the correct call for a test whose value depends on being stable.
  (b) The source-deletion half of R2's brief ("a skipped import must not delete its source")
      IS covered by separate, currently-passing tests:
        - internal/handlers/explorer/handlers_test.go:2418-2448
          TestHandleImport_CollisionSkipsAndKeepsSource — goes through handleImport, which
          uses the UNSTUBBED production deps (`grep -n "defaultImportDeps()" handlers.go`
          -> :605, inside handleImport), posts delete_source=true (:2427), and asserts
          Status=="skipped" (:2433), !SourceDeleted (:2436), mustExist(src) (:2440) and that
          the destination was not overwritten (:2445). This is the step-3 fast-path skip.
        - internal/handlers/explorer/handlers_test.go:2576-2612
          TestImportOneFile_ConcurrentCreateBetweenCheckAndWriteSkipsWithoutOverwrite —
          deleteSource=true (:2592), asserts !SourceDeleted (:2599) and mustExist(src)
          (:2611). This is the os.ErrExist skip branch, but with a stubbed write.
      Control flow confirms the two are exhaustive: `grep -n removeSrc handlers.go` gives one
      call site, :725, reached only after Status was set to "imported"; both skip returns
      (:681 fast path, :700 ErrExist) return earlier.
  (c) Residual, for the lead's awareness only — not a criterion failure and not something
      R8 was asked to do: no test exercises the combination "ErrExist skip branch + delete
      source enabled + unstubbed production binding". That cell is covered by the stubbed
      test (b) only. Per (a) it cannot be covered by a deterministic concurrency test of this
      shape. If the lead wants it closed it needs a different design (e.g. pre-creating the
      destination and driving a single import with delete_source=true), which is a new task.

